.TH GAMBITREE 1
.SH NAME
gambitree \- open and explore chess games
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gambitree [OPTIONS] [FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gambitree opens chess game files (PGN files) and shows the moves beside a
chessboard. You can replay the moves, read notes, and explore other possible
lines without changing the game file.
.PP
A file can contain one game or many games. You can also open several files in
one window. Gambitree keeps the files in the order you give them.
.SH QUICK START
Open one game file from a terminal:
.PP
.RS
.B gambitree /path/to/my-game.pgn
.RE
.PP
Use the Right Arrow key to play through the game. The board shows the position
after the selected move. To go back, use the Left Arrow key. You can also click
a move in the game or an alternative line to show that position.
.PP
To open more than one file, list their names in order:
.PP
.RS
.B gambitree tournament.pgn another-game.pgn
.RE
.PP
Use Page Down and Page Up to move between games in the current file. Use
Control-Page Down and Control-Page Up to move between files.
Use Control-G to choose a game from a searchable list. Use Control-Shift-G to
choose a file given when Gambitree started. Type to filter, use Up/Down to
select and Enter to open. Escape closes either list without changing the game.
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROLS
.TP
.B ?
Show or hide a quick guide to the keyboard controls inside the main window.
Escape also closes the guide. Game controls are inactive while it is open.
Use Up/Down, Page Up/Down, Home/End or the mouse wheel to scroll it.
.TP
.B Control-F
Open the notation search bar. Type to search moves, alternatives and comments.
Press Enter or F3 for the next match; hold Shift for the previous match.
Search is case-insensitive and wraps at the beginning and end. Escape closes
the search bar without changing the selected chess move.
.TP
.B Right Arrow
Go to the next move in the current line. At the end of a line, the selection
stays on the last move.
.TP
.B Left Arrow
Go back one move. If you go back across the first move of an alternative line,
Gambitree returns to the position where that line began. Right Arrow then
continues along the played game or the selected parent line.
.TP
.B Up Arrow
Leave the alternative line you are exploring and return to the position where
it branches from the line above it. In a nested alternative, press Up again to
leave the next level. Up has no effect on the played game when no alternative
line is open.
.TP
.B Down Arrow
Select the next alternative move offered at the same point, when one is
available. This works both in the played game and inside an alternative line.
It does not wrap to earlier choices; click an earlier move to select it.
.PP
If there is no next choice inside an alternative line, skip the rest of that
line and select the next move shown after it. This may be a move in the line
above or the first move of another alternative. If neither action has a
destination, the selection stays where it is.
.TP
.B Home
Show the starting position of the current game.
.TP
.B End
Go to the last move reached by following the current line and any choices you
have selected. Where no choice was made, Gambitree follows the move played in
the game. The selected move remains visible. Text after the game does not move
the selection away from the last chess move.
.TP
.B Page Up / Page Down
Show the previous or next game in the current file.
.TP
.B Control-G
Open a searchable list of games in the current file. Type to filter, use
Up/Down to select and Enter to open a game. Escape closes the list without
changing the current game.
.TP
.B Control-Shift-G
Open a searchable list of PGN files given when Gambitree started. Type to
filter, use Up/Down to select and Enter to open a file. Escape cancels.
.TP
.B Shift-Page Up / Shift-Page Down
Scroll the notation up or down by about one visible screen, keeping the selected
move and board position unchanged. A small overlap keeps your place in the text.
.TP
.B Control-Page Up / Control-Page Down
Show the previous or next file.
.TP
.B Control-Delete
Open a confirmation to permanently remove the selected game from its file. This
cannot be undone. See the section Removing a game below.
.TP
.B F
Turn the chessboard around.
.TP
.B B
Cycle through Classic, Wood and Wood Veneer boards. Classic remains the default.
Gambitree remembers the choice independently of the piece set.
Wood Veneer uses two calm beige/brown texture images, with a different fixed
crop for each logical square. Resizing preserves the crop; rotation turns it
with the board. A cached background avoids texture scaling during ordinary
redraws. Missing veneer assets fall back to classic board colours.
The supplied veneer images are original AI-generated alternatives, not real
photographs or third-party CC0 scans. Full provenance remains in the source
distribution; installed asset credits are in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.
.TP
.B P
Cycle through Gambitree Classic, Meridian Shaded and Cburnett pieces.
Gambitree remembers the choice independently of the board theme.
.TP
.B G
Switch between piece letters and chess figurines in the notation and engine
move line. Letter
notation is the default. Gambitree remembers your choice for the next start.
.TP
.B + / -
Make the move text larger or smaller. The equals key also increases the size.
Hold Control while pressing these keys to change only the engine analysis
text size. Each size is remembered, from 8 to 24 points. Ordinary interface
text keeps its size.
.TP
.B A
Turn chess engine analysis on or off. Stockfish is used by default when it is
available.
.TP
.B Escape / Q
Close Gambitree. When the keyboard guide is open, Escape closes only the guide
and Q is ignored.
.SH EXPLORING ALTERNATIVE MOVES
An alternative line is another possible sequence of moves shown in parentheses
in the notation. To explore one:
.IP "1." 4
Find the alternative moves in the notation and click a move in that line.
.IP "2." 4
Press Right Arrow to continue through that line.
.IP "3." 4
Press Up Arrow to return to the position where the alternative began.
.IP "4." 4
Press Right Arrow to continue with the played game.
.PP
Up Arrow returns to the position where the current alternative line begins.
Down Arrow first selects the next alternative move at the same point, even
inside another alternative. If there is no next choice, it skips the rest of
the current alternative line and selects the next move shown after it. If
neither action has a destination, the selection stays where it is. Click an
earlier move to select it again.
.PP
For example, an annotated game such as Byrne--Fischer may show several
alternative lines. Explore one with Right Arrow, then use Up Arrow to return
and continue the played game. At the end of a line, Right Arrow stays at its
last move; use Up Arrow to leave an alternative line or Left Arrow to go back.
.SH MOUSE CONTROLS
.TP
.B Left mouse button on a move
Show the position after that move and select it in the notation.
.TP
.B Mouse wheel
Scroll the notation. Scrolling does not change the selected chess move.
.TP
.B Top controls
Use the controls at the top of the side panel to move between loaded files and
games.
.PP
Drag the divider between the board and notation to give either panel more
space. Gambitree remembers your chosen proportions for the next start and
adjusts them when the window size changes.
.PP
Annotations use symbols: a right arrow marks attack, equals/infinity signs
mark compensation, a double-headed arrow marks counterplay, and a circled dot
marks zugzwang. Counterplay is slight when the arrow has a plus sign and
moderate otherwise. A clockwise arrow marks a development advantage; a circled
plus marks time pressure. For these last two symbols, one or two following
plus signs mean slight or moderate respectively.
Delta means "with the idea" and inverted delta means
"aimed against". Comparison symbols indicate better, worse or equivalent
continuations. These are editor annotations, not engine scores. "W" means White
and "Z" means Black. NAG codes 149 and above are kept in the PGN data but are
not shown in the notation.
.SH OPENING GAME FILES
Give one or more file paths when starting Gambitree.  For example:
.PP
.RS
.B gambitree 'My chess games.pgn'
.RE
.PP
If a file contains several games, Page Up and Page Down move between them.
When several files are open, Control-Page Up and Control-Page Down move
between files. The program reads games for viewing and does not change the
PGN files when you view them. Permanent removal is a separate command,
described under Removing a game below.
The Control-G list makes it easier to jump directly to a game. The
Control-Shift-G list shows the startup file arguments in their original order.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --fullscreen
Ask the window manager to start fullscreen. Not saved in session settings.
.TP
.B --iconic
Ask the window manager to start minimized. Not saved in session settings.
.TP
.BI --title " TITLE"
Use TITLE as the window title.  Without this option, the usual Gambitree
title, filename and available game index are shown.
.TP
.BI --role " NAME"
Set the main window's WM_WINDOW_ROLE property for window-manager rules.
NAME must be non-empty printable ASCII.  By default this property is absent.
.TP
.BI --engine " PATH"
Use PATH as the chess engine program instead of the default name
.BR stockfish .
.TP
.BI --board " BOARD"
Choose classic, wood or wood-veneer. This overrides the saved board preference
and is remembered through the usual session settings. Without this option,
use the saved preference, or Classic if none is available. If repeated, the
last value wins. Press B to cycle boards while the window is open.
.TP
.BI --geometry " GEOMETRY"
Set the starting client size and window position using X11 geometry syntax,
for example 1200x800+40+60, 1200x800, or +40+60. Negative offsets such as
1200x800-20-20 measure from the right and bottom edges; -0 preserves this
edge-relative meaning. Unspecified components use the saved geometry.
Explicit dimensions are limited to 480x320 through 8192x8192 and fitted to
the screen, even if it is smaller than the minimum. Off-screen positions
are brought back onto the screen. Window-manager placement policies apply.
Missing or invalid values are ignored with a warning; the last valid
--geometry wins. The resulting geometry is remembered through the existing
session settings.
.TP
.BI --pieces " NAME"
Choose a bundled piece design:
gambitree, \%meridian\-shaded or cburnett.
The name classic also selects the Gambitree design; names of locally installed
piece folders may also be used.
.TP
.B --print-window-id
Print the X11 window number after the window opens.
.TP
.B --help
Show a short list of command options and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Show the Gambitree version and exit.
.SH ADVANCED DETAILS
.SS Games with a different starting position
Gambitree supports standard chess games that use a FEN starting position,
including positions with missing pieces. Castling rights must agree with the
king and rook positions. PGN Variant values Chess960, Fischerandom, and Fischer
Random select Chess960 rules before reading the starting position. Chess960
FEN accepts X-FEN castling symbols KQkq and Shredder-FEN rook-file letters A-H
and a-h. Unknown Variant values are reported as errors.
.SS Removing a game
Control-Delete opens a confirmation dialog for permanently removing the
selected game from its PGN file. This cannot be undone. It is available only
after the file has finished indexing, and only for a regular, non-symbolic-link
file no larger than 32 MiB with one hard link. Gambitree records a SHA-256
content fingerprint and file identity when a file is loaded; removal is refused
if the identity, metadata, or content has changed.
.PP
Larger PGN files remain available for viewing, but game removal is disabled
for files over the limit. Gambitree stages the remaining file contents in the
same folder and publishes them by rename. It does not create a backup. A
non-cooperating external writer can still change the file during the final
verification and replacement. After removal, Gambitree selects the next game
in the file, or the previous game if there is no next game. Removing a file's
only game also removes that file from disk and from the loaded file list.
.SS Engine analysis
After the window opens, Gambitree starts Stockfish asynchronously when it is
available on PATH. It shows the search depth, score from White's point of view,
and a suggested move line. A missing or failed engine does not prevent viewing
games. For Chess960 games Gambitree enables Stockfish's UCI_Chess960 setting
and updates it when changing game types.
Selective depth (sel) indicates the maximum selective search depth reported
by the engine, measured in plies.
Each Black move in the suggested line is followed by a period, without move
numbers. Click a suggested move to preview the position after it. The selected
analysis snapshot stays visible while Stockfish continues searching the original
PGN position. PGN navigation (including an unsuccessful navigation attempt) or
clicking a PGN move ends the preview and shows current analysis again. Preview
moves never become part of the PGN game and have no keyboard navigation.
.SS Remembered settings
Changed settings are saved after 1000 milliseconds without another settings
change. Resizing postpones the write until the geometry settles; navigation,
scrolling and engine output do not postpone it. Unchanged values are not written.
Normal exit and SIGTERM flush pending changes immediately. SIGKILL and crashes
cannot do so. Gambitree saves the window size, board theme, piece design,
analysis setting, figurines, text sizes, panel proportions and the window
position when the window manager reports it reliably.
An off-screen saved position is adjusted so part
of the window remains visible; the window manager may choose another position.
.PP
Settings are stored in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/gambitree/state
when XDG_STATE_HOME is an absolute path. Otherwise Gambitree uses
.I ~/.local/state/gambitree/state
when HOME is available. If neither location is usable, settings are not saved.
An invalid or unwritable settings file does not prevent normal use.
.PP
Settings are replaced atomically. Cooperating processes merge their own changed
fields with the latest file under a non-blocking lock; a process with no changes
does not overwrite newer settings at exit. For conflicting edits to the same
field, the last successful save wins. Write errors retain pending changes,
warn once and retry after a delay. An unreadable state file is not blindly
replaced. A small state.lock file is retained beside the state file.
.SS Large files
One selected game may use up to 64 MiB when loaded. For conventional PGN
databases of 128 MiB or more, Gambitree builds a provisional index in 1 MiB
blocks and continues while the window remains usable. Other input is indexed
with a general scanner. A physical input line longer than 1 MiB is rejected to
limit memory use.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B The window opens but no game is shown.
Start Gambitree with a game file path, for example
.BR "gambitree /path/to/my-game.pgn" .
.TP
.B Stockfish is not available.
You can still view and replay games. Install Stockfish or use
.B --engine PATH
to name another UCI chess engine.
.TP
.B I reached the end of an alternative line.
Press Up Arrow to return to the line above it. Press Left Arrow to go back one
move, or click a move in another line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GAMBITREE_DIAGNOSTICS=1
Write indexing, memory, parsing, engine, and rendering details to standard
error. This also prints the window number to standard output.
.SH FILES
Installed piece designs are below share/gambitree/pieces. Application icons
use share/icons/hicolor. Development builds on Linux also search for icon
files relative to /proc/self/exe. The classic board appearance is drawn by the
program. LICENSE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES are installed below share/doc/gambitree.
Piece-design sources, hashes and full provenance remain in the source
distribution, as described in assets/README.md.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The main window exposes WM_CLASS, _NET_WM_PID, WM_COMMAND, WM_CLIENT_MACHINE,
and STOCKFISH_PID while an engine process exists.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means normal completion. Invalid command-line options exit with status 2.
Startup or X11 errors exit with a non-zero status.
